 # Chunk-based spatial partitioning
 
-
 ## Overview
-Chunk-based spatial partitioning divides a volumetric dataset into fixed-size spatial subvolumes (chunks or bricks) to manage memory, enable out-of-core streaming, and facilitate parallel processing [G]. It solves the problem of handling datasets larger than available main memory or GPU VRAM by ensuring only relevant chunks reside in active memory [2369-chunk-based-world-streaming.md]. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its reliance on fixed-size spatial blocks as the atomic unit of data management, contrasting with [Sectioning](1636-sectioning.md)'s view-dependent slicing, [Uniform Block Partitioning with Acceleration](6098-uniform-block-partitioning-with-acceleration.md)'s focus on local acceleration within a single context, and [Distributed computing and load balancing](5624-distributed-computing-and-load-balancing.md)'s network-distributed strategies [G].
+Chunk-based spatial partitioning divides a volumetric dataset into fixed-size spatial subvolumes (chunks or bricks) to manage memory, enable out-of-core streaming, and facilitate parallel processing [G]. It solves the problem of handling datasets larger than available main memory or GPU VRAM by ensuring only relevant chunks reside in active memory [G]. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its reliance on fixed-size spatial blocks as the atomic unit of data management, contrasting with [Sectioning](1636-sectioning.md)'s view-dependent slicing, [Uniform Block Partitioning with Acceleration](6098-uniform-block-partitioning-with-acceleration.md)'s focus on local acceleration within a single context, and [Distributed computing and load balancing](5624-distributed-computing-and-load-balancing.md)'s network-distributed strategies [G].
 
