 ## Background
-The concept of partitioning volume data into manageable chunks for parallel processing or out-of-core storage has been fundamental to scalable volume rendering [G]. Early architectures like COLOSSUS demonstrated the feasibility of dividing datasets into subvolumes for parallel ray processing, highlighting the need for boundary handling to avoid artifacts [S1]. Later developments, such as the Moving Walls Algorithm, introduced dynamic partitioning for sparse data on clusters [9715-moving-walls-algorithm.md]. The rise of large-scale scientific datasets (e.g., BigBrain) and interactive applications (e.g., voxel games) has driven the evolution of chunk-based strategies to include neural training [3168-chunk-based-training.md], hierarchical terrain rendering [4050-chunk-trees.md], and residency-aware streaming [10150-tide-trajectory-adaptive-differential-streaming.md].
